      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We start with the given polynomial equation \\(3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0\\). The roots of this polynomial are \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\). According to Vieta's formulas, for a cubic polynomial \\(Ax^3 + Bx^2 + Cx + D = 0\\), the sum of the roots taken one at a time (i.e., \\(a + b + c\\)) is given by \\(-\\frac{B}{A}\\), the sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time (i.e., \\(ab + bc + ca\\)) is given by \\(\\frac{C}{A}\\), and the product of the roots taken three at a time (i.e., \\(abc\\)) is given by \\(-\\frac{D}{A}\\).\n\nFor the polynomial \\(3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0\\), we have:\n- \\(A = 3\\),\n- \\(B = -3\\),\n- \\(C = 11\\),\n- \\(D = -8\\).\n\nUsing Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots \\(a + b + c\\) is:\n\\[\na + b + c = -\\frac{B}{A} = -\\frac{-3}{3} = 1.\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time \\(ab + bc + ca\\) is:\n\\[\nab + bc + ca = \\frac{C}{A} = \\frac{11}{3}.\n\\]\n\nWe are asked to find \\(ab + ac + bc\\). From the formula \\(ab + bc + ca\\), we see that \\(ab + bc + ca\\) is already given as:\n\\[\nab + bc + ca = \\frac{11}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(ab + ac + bc\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{11}{3}}\\).",
